        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is water where the floor meets the wall a serious problem?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not structurally. The cove joint is a normal construction seam, and seepage through it rarely means the foundation is failing. It is still worth fixing promptly, because repeated wetting ruins flooring, stored belongings and finishes, and it feeds mould. The exception is seepage alongside a horizontal crack or a wall bowing inward, which calls for a structural engineer rather than a waterproofer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I just seal the cove joint myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, and for minor dampness on a poured wall that appears only in the heaviest storms, a flexible polyurethane sealant is a reasonable short-term fix once the gutters and grading are sorted. For water that actually runs, seeps along several walls or comes back after sealing, the joint is only the outlet, and sealing it usually pushes the water somewhere else."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does it only leak after heavy rain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Because the soil around the foundation only saturates after heavy or sustained rain. A light shower soaks into the top few inches and never reaches the footing. When the ground does saturate, the water table rises and pushes on the joint. A leak within an hour of rain usually points to roof water or grading; one that arrives many hours later points to groundwater."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is the joint wet when it has not rained?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "There are three likely reasons. In humid summer weather it is often condensation on cold concrete, which the plastic sheet test will confirm. In low-lying areas the water table can stay high for weeks after a wet spell or during snowmelt. And occasionally it is a plumbing leak, from a supply line under the slab or a nearby appliance, which is worth ruling out."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a dehumidifier stop water at the cove joint?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if the water is condensation. A dehumidifier holding the basement near 50 percent humidity stops moisture condensing on cold concrete, and it helps dry the space out after any leak. It does nothing about water entering from outside, whether that is pushed up through the joint or draining out of a block wall, and running it harder does not reduce groundwater pressure at all."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does it cost to fix a leak at the cove joint?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends entirely on the cause. Gutter, downspout and grading fixes typically cost a few hundred dollars at most. A sealant for minor seepage is under $60 in materials. A professional interior perimeter drain with a sump commonly runs from around $4,500 on a small basement to well over $15,000 on a large one, and exterior excavation usually costs considerably more."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need an interior drain on every wall or just the leaking one?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If seepage is confined to one wall and the timing points to a local surface water source, fixing that source and draining only that wall can be enough. If water comes in along several walls, or the house sits on clay or in a high water table area, a partial system often just moves the leak to the undrained walls, so a full perimeter is usually the better spend."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
