        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Water along the line where the basement floor meets the wall is the most common basement leak there is, and the one most often fixed wrong. The seam was never sealed in the first place, the water reaching it is almost always coming from somewhere else, and filling the gap from inside tends to move the leak a few feet rather than stop it. Work out which of three causes you have first; the right fix, and the right budget, follows from that.
        </p>

        <div id="why-the-joint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Water Finds the Cove Joint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most basements are built in three pours, not one. The footing goes in first, a wide strip of concrete at the bottom of the excavation. The walls go on top of it, either poured concrete or stacked block. Then, often weeks later and after the under-floor plumbing is roughed in, the floor slab is poured inside the walls, butting up against the footing and the base of the wall. Where the new slab meets the older concrete there is a seam called the cove joint, and because the two pours never bonded, it is what builders call a cold joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            A cold joint is not a defect. It is how nearly every basement in North America is built, and on a dry day the gap is usually a hairline you would never notice. The slab also shrinks slightly as it cures and pulls away from the wall by a fraction of an inch in places. Nobody seals it, because on a site with good drainage nothing ever comes through. But it is the one continuous opening that runs the full perimeter of the basement at its lowest point, and water always takes the easiest path available.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the soil around and under the house saturates after heavy rain or snowmelt, the water in it has weight. Every foot of standing water adds roughly 0.43 pounds per square inch of pressure at the bottom, so a water table sitting four feet above the footing is pressing on the base of the foundation with close to 2 psi, which is about 250 pounds on every square foot. Solid concrete can resist that. A seam between two separate pours cannot, and the water comes up through the joint and spreads across the floor. Our guide to <a href="/hydrostatic-pressure-in-basement/">hydrostatic pressure in a basement</a> covers the physics in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the leak shows up at the joint even when the actual water problem is thirty feet away at a downspout, or under a patio that has tilted toward the house. The joint is not the cause; it is the outlet. Everything that follows depends on keeping those two ideas separate, because almost every dollar wasted on this problem is spent treating the outlet while the cause carries on untouched.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also explains why seepage often starts years after the house was built. Gutters clog, a downspout extension gets knocked off by a mower, the backfill next to the foundation settles into a shallow moat, and an original footing drain slowly silts up. The house did not change; the water around it did. A basement that used to be dry and has started seeping at the cove joint is usually telling you something changed outside, which is good news, because outside changes tend to be the cheapest ones to reverse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="three-sources" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Three Leaks That Look Identical
          </h2>

          <p>
            A wet line along the base of the wall looks the same whatever produced it. In practice it comes from one of three places, and they need completely different fixes. Mixing them up is how people end up paying for a drainage system to fix a humidity problem, or squeezing a twelve-dollar tube of caulk at a rising water table.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Pressure from below</h3>

          <p>
            This is the case described above: saturated soil pushing water up through the seam between slab and footing. The signature is water that appears at the joint first, often at several points at once, while the wall above it stays dry. It follows sustained rain rather than a quick shower, frequently with a lag of several hours to a day, and in a bad case you can see it seeping steadily out of the seam. Damp patches often appear around slab cracks or the floor drain at the same time, which is the territory of our guide to <a href="/water-coming-through-basement-floor/">water coming through the basement floor</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Water running down inside the wall</h3>

          <p>
            Concrete block walls are hollow. Each block has two or three vertical cores, and when water gets into a block wall through a crack, a failed mortar joint or simply by soaking through the porous outer face, it drains down inside those cores. At the bottom it has nowhere to go but out, so it exits at the lowest course, right at or just above the cove joint. The signature is dampness or dark staining on the lower courses, white mineral deposits called efflorescence on the face of the block, and water that appears at the base of the wall fairly quickly after rain. Poured walls can do a version of this through a vertical crack that carries water down to the floor. Block walls have their own guide in <a href="/waterproofing-cinder-block-basement-walls/">waterproofing cinder block basement walls</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Condensation that pools</h3>

          <p>
            This is the one that fools people most often. The bottom of the wall and the edge of the slab are the coldest surfaces in the basement because they are the deepest below ground. In summer, warm humid air drifts down, meets that cold concrete, drops below its dew point and condenses, and the water runs down and collects in exactly the line where a leak would. The signature is dampness that turns up in muggy weather whether or not it has rained, a film of moisture spread evenly along the base rather than concentrated at points, and a basement that feels clammy. No drain in the world fixes it, because nothing is coming in from outside.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Source</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">When it shows up</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Wall above the joint</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What actually fixes it</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pressure from below</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hours after sustained rain or thaw; lingers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grading first, then interior perimeter drain and sump</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water down the wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soon after rain; fades as it dries</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Damp lower courses, efflorescence</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gutters and grading, then drain with weep holes or exterior work</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Condensation</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Humid weather, rain or not</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Evenly damp film, cold to touch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dehumidifier and keeping humid air out</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            It is common to have two at once. A block-walled house on heavy clay can have water in the cores and pressure under the slab in the same storm, and a basement with a small real leak also tends to be humid, which adds condensation on top. The diagnosis below is about finding the dominant cause, since that is where the money should go first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Tell Which One You Have
          </h2>

          <p>
            You can usually sort this out over a week or two with a notebook, a roll of duct tape and some plastic sheeting. It is worth doing before calling anyone out, because a contractor who sells drainage systems will, reasonably enough, tend to see a drainage problem, and a salesperson for coatings will tend to see a coating problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch the timing</h3>

          <p>
            Note when the water appears relative to the rain. Water that shows up within an hour or two of a downpour and stops soon after points to surface water, either running down the outside of the wall or entering through block. Water that arrives many hours after sustained rain, or during spring thaw, and lingers for days points to a rising water table and pressure from below. Dampness that appears in sticky July weather with no rain at all, and vanishes when the air turns dry, points to condensation. Water that is present constantly, in every season, needs a closer look at the water table and at the plumbing, including any supply line running under the slab.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Run the plastic sheet test</h3>

          <p>
            Tape a square of clear plastic, around 18 inches on a side, flat against the wall just above the joint, and another on the floor beside it, sealing all four edges of each. Leave them for 24 to 48 hours. Moisture on the underside, between the plastic and the concrete, means water is coming through the concrete from outside. Moisture on the room side of the plastic means it is condensing out of the basement air. Aluminium foil works just as well. A mix of both results tells you that both are happening, which is more common than people expect.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the wall above the joint</h3>

          <p>
            After a rain, run the back of your hand down the wall from about two feet up to the floor. If the wall is dry all the way down and the water is only at the seam, it is coming up from below. If the lower courses of block are damp, darker or wet to the touch, and the wetness gets worse towards the floor, water is coming down through the wall. On a block wall you can confirm it by drilling a small hole into a core near the bottom of a damp course: if water trickles out, the cores are holding water. Plug the hole afterwards or leave it for whoever installs the drainage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, map where along the perimeter it happens. Seepage concentrated under one downspout, beside a sloping patio or at a corner where the ground dips is almost always a surface water problem on that side of the house. Seepage along every wall at once, especially in a house on clay or in a low part of the neighbourhood, is much more likely to be groundwater. A cheap <a href="/basement-water-alarm/">basement water alarm</a> set on the floor at the worst spot tells you exactly when it starts, which is the single most useful piece of data most homeowners never have.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Short Version</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Dry wall, wet joint: from below. Wet wall, wet joint: from above. Wet plastic on the room side: from the air.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Those three observations settle the diagnosis in most basements, and each one points to a different fix with a very different price tag. Spend a week on them before spending anything else.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="outside-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Fix the Outside First: Gutters, Downspouts, Grading
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whatever the diagnosis says, the first fixes are the same, and they cost next to nothing compared with anything that involves cutting concrete. For the two causes that involve rain, they take a large share of the water away before it gets anywhere near the joint. They sit at the top of every honest ranking of <a href="/wet-basement-solutions/">wet basement solutions</a> for the same reason: they deal with the cause rather than the outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the gutters. One inch of rain on 1,000 square feet of roof produces about 620 gallons of water, and the gutters collect all of it and deliver it to a handful of points at the corners of the house. A clogged gutter overflows and sheets that water straight down against the foundation. A downspout that empties onto the ground at the base of the wall is, in effect, a device for concentrating hundreds of gallons at the exact spot you least want them. Clean the gutters, stand outside in a hard rain to confirm they are not overflowing, and extend every downspout at least 4 feet from the foundation, or 10 feet where the yard allows. Extensions typically cost $10 to $20 each.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then the grading. The ground next to the house should fall away from it, and the usual building code guideline is about 6 inches of drop over the first 10 feet. Over the decades, backfill settles and the soil beside the foundation sinks, so many older houses now have ground that slopes toward the wall and forms a shallow channel that collects every storm. Rebuilding the slope with compacted soil, ideally something with enough clay in it to shed water rather than soak it up, is a weekend with a wheelbarrow and a few cubic yards of fill. Keep the finished soil level at least 6 inches below siding and any wood framing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the hard surfaces too. A patio, walk or driveway that has tilted toward the house funnels water to the wall along its whole length, and the gap between that slab and the foundation is an open invitation. Sealing that exterior gap with a flexible outdoor sealant is a genuinely good use of caulk, unlike the interior version discussed below. Window wells should have a gravel base, be clear of leaves and ideally have covers, because a well that fills up becomes a bucket held against the wall.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter Outside</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">620 gal</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Runoff from 1 in of rain on 1,000 sq ft of roof</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">4-10 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Downspout discharge distance from the wall</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Grade drop over the first 10 ft</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.43 psi</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pressure added per foot of groundwater</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Then wait for a real storm or two before deciding anything else. If the seepage stops or drops to a trace, you are finished, and you have spent less than a couple of hundred dollars. If it carries on unchanged, especially along several walls and many hours after the rain, you have learned something just as useful: the water is coming from the ground rather than the roof, and the next steps are about relieving pressure rather than redirecting runoff.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sealing the Joint From Inside: What It Can and Cannot Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the fix most people try first, because it is cheap and it looks like it addresses the problem head-on: there is a gap, water comes through the gap, so fill the gap. The trouble is that sealing the joint from inside means fighting water pressure at the point where the pressure is highest, using a material stuck to the inside surface of a seam that the water is pushing on from the outside and from below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydraulic cement is the usual candidate. It sets in minutes, expands slightly as it cures and will even set underwater, which makes it genuinely useful for plugging an actively leaking hole or a tie-rod opening, as our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-hydraulic-cement/">how to use hydraulic cement</a> explains. On a cove joint it has two problems. It is rigid, while the slab and wall move independently with temperature and moisture, so the bond tends to crack within a season or two. And even when it holds, it does nothing to reduce the pressure behind it. The water that used to come through the joint simply moves to the next weakest point: a shrinkage crack in the slab, a stretch of joint a few feet along that you did not fill, the floor drain or a pipe penetration.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the pattern people describe again and again: seal the joint along one wall, and the next storm puts water in the middle of the floor. Nothing got worse, exactly; the same water found a new door. On a block wall it can do real harm, because sealing the bottom of the wall blocks the exit for water draining down the cores. The water level rises inside the block, pushes out through mortar joints higher up and leaves the wall wetter than it was before.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a fair short-term case for a flexible polyurethane sealant of the kind sold for concrete joints and cracks. It stays elastic as the slab moves, and some formulations are made to bond to damp concrete. If you have minor seepage on a poured wall, meaning a damp line rather than running water, that appears only in the worst storms and that you have already reduced by fixing gutters and grading, sealing the joint can stop that last trace. Clean the joint back to sound concrete, let it dry as far as it will, push backer rod into any gap wider than about a quarter of an inch, and expect to redo it in a few years. Our sibling guide to <a href="/caulk-for-basement-wall-cracks/">caulk for basement wall cracks</a> covers which sealant types are worth buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            What sealing is not is a fix for pressure-driven water. If water comes through the joint in a visible flow, or seeps along several walls at once, sealing buys a season at best and a new leak elsewhere at worst. Crack injection, which works well on individual cracks in a poured wall as our <a href="/basement-crack-repair/">basement crack repair</a> guide describes, is rarely a durable answer here either: there is no enclosed crack for the resin to fill from face to face, only an open seam running along a footing that the water can travel around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="interior-drain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Interior Perimeter Drain and Sump: The Fix That Holds
          </h2>

          <p>
            For pressure-driven seepage, the fix that works does the opposite of sealing. Instead of trying to block water at the joint, it gives the water an easier path than the joint, below the level of the slab, and then removes it. That is an interior perimeter drain, also called interior drain tile or an interior French drain, and it is the standard professional answer to a cove joint leak for good reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            The installer breaks out a strip of floor, usually around a foot wide, along the inside of the affected walls. They dig down beside the footing, lay perforated pipe in a bed of washed stone and pitch it toward a sump pit, where a pump lifts the water out and discharges it away from the house. A dimpled plastic membrane or flange is normally set against the base of the wall to catch water coming down its face and steer it into the stone. Then the strip of floor is re-poured. Because the pipe sits below the underside of the slab, the rising water table reaches the pipe first and is drained away before it can build pressure under the floor. The joint stays dry because there is nothing left pushing on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a block wall the installer also drills a weep hole into the bottom of each core, so water draining down inside the wall empties into the drain rather than out at the joint. This is why an interior drain is usually the right answer for block walls leaking at the base, even when the source is surface water: it accepts that the cores will carry some water and gives that water somewhere to go. How the whole system fits together, from trench to discharge line, is laid out in our guide to the <a href="/basement-drainage-system/">basement drainage system</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sump pump becomes the part of the house that keeps the basement dry, so it deserves real attention. It needs to be sized to your actual inflow at your actual discharge height rather than chosen by horsepower, which our guide on <a href="/what-size-sump-pump-do-i-need/">what size sump pump you need</a> walks through. It needs a battery or water-powered backup if the basement matters, because the storms that fill the pit are often the ones that cut the power. And the discharge line has to end far enough from the house that you are not simply recirculating the same water back to the foundation.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a cheaper variant worth knowing about: a plastic channel glued to the floor along the base of the wall, which collects water coming down the wall or out of block cores and carries it to a sump without breaking the slab. For block walls where the water genuinely comes from the wall, it can work, and it costs noticeably less. It does nothing for water under the slab, and if the adhesive lets go the water runs underneath it. Be sceptical of anyone selling it as the cure for water rising up through the joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            The limits of the full system are worth being clear about too. An interior drain manages water after it reaches the foundation; it does not keep the wall itself dry, so a block wall stays damp inside and may still want a coating or membrane before you finish the space, which our <a href="/interior-basement-waterproofing/">interior basement waterproofing</a> guide compares. It does nothing for condensation. And it adds a pump, a mechanical part that typically needs replacing every seven to ten years. For most cove joint leaks those are acceptable trade-offs, and our <a href="/best-basement-waterproofing-systems/">best basement waterproofing systems</a> guide shows how the main approaches stack up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="exterior-and-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Exterior Work, Condensation, and What Each Fix Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior waterproofing means excavating down to the footing on the outside, cleaning the wall, applying a waterproof membrane, laying new drain pipe along the footing and backfilling with gravel. It is the most complete fix for water coming through or down the wall, because it keeps water out of the concrete altogether, and it is the right call when a wall is also failing structurally or when you are digging anyway for an addition or an egress window. It is also typically two to four times the cost of an interior system, and it tears up everything along that wall, from planting to patios and decks.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a cove joint leak driven by the water table, exterior work is often the poorer value. The membrane protects the wall, but water rising under the slab is still rising under the slab, and unless the new footing drain has somewhere to discharge and stays clear, the pressure at the joint may barely change. Our comparison of <a href="/interior-vs-exterior-basement-waterproofing/">interior vs exterior basement waterproofing</a> goes through which problems each one suits.
          </p>

          <p>
            One exterior check is worth doing before any of that. Many houses were built with a drain running around the outside of the footing, and in older homes it is often clogged with silt or roots, or its outlet has been buried or crushed. If you can find a cleanout, a plumber with a sewer camera can inspect it for a few hundred dollars, and clearing or reconnecting an existing footing drain costs far less than building a new drainage system from scratch.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the diagnosis came back as condensation, none of the above applies. The fix is drier air: a dehumidifier set to around 50 percent relative humidity, draining to a floor drain or sump so it runs unattended, plus keeping basement windows shut in humid summer weather, since opening them lets in the very air that condenses. Insulating the rim joist and wrapping cold water pipes helps further. Our guide to <a href="/basement-moisture-control/">basement moisture control</a> covers sizing and placement.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fix</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Right call when</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gutter cleaning, downspout extensions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under $100 DIY</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Any seepage that follows rain</td>
                  </tr>
