                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Exterior excavation and membrane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often $15,000 and well beyond</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Structural wall problems, or already excavating</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Treat those as planning ranges rather than quotes. Soil, access, region and how much of the perimeter needs work move real numbers by thousands, which is why three written bids matter more here than on most home projects. The <a href="/basement-waterproofing-cost/">basement waterproofing cost</a> guide breaks the professional figures down by basement size, and any bid that jumps straight to the most expensive option without asking when the water appears deserves a second opinion.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking the joint before checking the gutters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most cove joint seepage that follows rain starts at a clogged gutter, a downspout dumping at the wall or soil sloping toward the house. Those fixes cost under a couple of hundred dollars and remove water at the source. Sealing the joint first treats the outlet, leaves the cause in place and usually just moves the leak across the floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Packing the joint with hydraulic cement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hydraulic cement is excellent for plugging an actively leaking hole and poor for a cove joint. It is rigid while the slab and wall move independently, so the bond tends to crack within a season or two. Even while it holds, it does nothing about the pressure behind it, and the water reappears at the next weak spot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a drainage system for a humidity problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Condensation collects at the base of the wall because that is the coldest concrete in the house, and it looks exactly like seepage. Run the plastic sheet test before paying for anything. If the moisture forms on the room side, a dehumidifier costing a few hundred dollars solves what a drain costing several thousand never could.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the bottom of a hollow block wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water running down inside block cores drains out at the lowest course. Seal that exit with a coating or sealant and the water backs up inside the wall, rising until it pushes through mortar joints higher up. The wall ends up wetter than before. Block walls need their cores drained, usually through weep holes into an interior drain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Finishing the basement after one dry month</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A basement that stayed dry through a mild spell has not been proven dry. After any fix, wait through a full wet season, including spring thaw and at least one heavy storm, before framing, insulating or laying flooring against the wall. Drywall and carpet hide a returning leak until mould and rot have already done the damage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is water where the floor meets the wall a serious problem?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not structurally. The cove joint is a normal construction seam, and seepage through it rarely means the foundation is failing. It is still worth fixing promptly, because repeated wetting ruins flooring, stored belongings and finishes, and it feeds mould. The exception is seepage alongside a horizontal crack or a wall bowing inward, which calls for a structural engineer rather than a waterproofer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I just seal the cove joint myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, and for minor dampness on a poured wall that appears only in the heaviest storms, a flexible polyurethane sealant is a reasonable short-term fix once the gutters and grading are sorted. For water that actually runs, seeps along several walls or comes back after sealing, the joint is only the outlet, and sealing it usually pushes the water somewhere else.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does it only leak after heavy rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because the soil around the foundation only saturates after heavy or sustained rain. A light shower soaks into the top few inches and never reaches the footing. When the ground does saturate, the water table rises and pushes on the joint. A leak within an hour of rain usually points to roof water or grading; one that arrives many hours later points to groundwater.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is the joint wet when it has not rained?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There are three likely reasons. In humid summer weather it is often condensation on cold concrete, which the plastic sheet test will confirm. In low-lying areas the water table can stay high for weeks after a wet spell or during snowmelt. And occasionally it is a plumbing leak, from a supply line under the slab or a nearby appliance, which is worth ruling out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier stop water at the cove joint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the water is condensation. A dehumidifier holding the basement near 50 percent humidity stops moisture condensing on cold concrete, and it helps dry the space out after any leak. It does nothing about water entering from outside, whether that is pushed up through the joint or draining out of a block wall, and running it harder does not reduce groundwater pressure at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to fix a leak at the cove joint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends entirely on the cause. Gutter, downspout and grading fixes typically cost a few hundred dollars at most. A sealant for minor seepage is under $60 in materials. A professional interior perimeter drain with a sump commonly runs from around $4,500 on a small basement to well over $15,000 on a large one, and exterior excavation usually costs considerably more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an interior drain on every wall or just the leaking one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If seepage is confined to one wall and the timing points to a local surface water source, fixing that source and draining only that wall can be enough. If water comes in along several walls, or the house sits on clay or in a high water table area, a partial system often just moves the leak to the undrained walls, so a full perimeter is usually the better spend.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

