        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Water at the cove joint is almost never a problem with the joint. It is water from somewhere else using the lowest, easiest opening in the basement. Work out whether it is coming up from below, down through the wall or out of the air, fix the gutters, downspouts and grading regardless, and give that a storm or two before spending more.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If it is still coming in, and especially if it arrives along several walls hours after the rain, stop trying to seal it and relieve the pressure instead. An interior perimeter drain with a properly sized sump is the fix that holds for most houses, and it costs a fraction of digging up the outside.
          </p>
